s Blasts / Secondary AML", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.08797262503906202, "posterior": 0.130735303620564, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Subacute presentation is not inconsistent with MDS/secondary AML, though does not strongly support it; this maintains its moderate_for status relative to de no…"], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.75, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "etiology"}}, "frontier": ["B1.1", "B1.3", "B1.1", "B1.3"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "What evidence is MOST INCONSISTENT with De Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML)? Identify the single strongest finding that argues against this diagnosis.", "summary": "The strongest evidence against de novo AML is the absence of other hallmark features like marrow fibrosis or prior myeloproliferative history, but pancytopenia with blasts remains more typical of AML than other alternatives."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of 35% peripheral blasts and pancytopenia (hemoglobin 10 g/dL, platelets 109,000/mm³) strongly support de novo AML (B1.1) over other myeloid or lymphoid malignancies in this subacut…", "summary": "The presence of 35% peripheral blasts and pancytopenia (anemia, thrombocytopenia) in this subacute presentation strongly supports de novo acute myeloid leukemia (AML, B1.1) as the most likely diagnosis. These findings are less consistent with lymphoid malignancies (e.g., ALL, B2) or chronic myelopr…"}, {"t": 4, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of 35% peripheral blasts and subacute constitutional symptoms (fever, weight loss) argue for de novo AML (B1.1) over CML-BC (B1.2) despite the pancytopenia?", "